The "from scratch" boundary. You write attention, LayerNorm, the MLP, embeddings, the KV cache, sampling and every quantizer. Engine code never imports a Hugging Face model class.
Which AI agent to use. The repo, not the tool, holds the plan, so any agent works. Put the rules in SPEC.md, then add one-line AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md and GEMINI.md files that say "read SPEC.md first".
Agent
Best use here
Antigravity
Scaffolding, test boilerplate, benchmark scripts, when quota allows
Codex
Bounded tasks written from a spec: tests, refactors, CI file
Claude (Claude Code or this chat)
Review, debugging logit mismatches, explaining concepts, report editing

Working rules with AI agents
You write the core math; agents write scaffolding, tests and plots; you read every diff before it is committed. Interviewers will ask about the core code, so it has to be yours.
You write
The agent may write
Attention, LayerNorm, MLP, embeddings
Repo scaffold, pyproject or requirements
KV cache and generation loop
Pytest boilerplate from your test spec
Every quantizer and the rescaling fix
Benchmark scripts and plotting code
Outlier analysis and the report's discussion
README formatting, CI YAML

A good test of ownership: if you cannot explain a function without opening it, rewrite it from memory.
Prompt template for each phase (paste into any agent):
Read SPEC.md first.
Task: <one phase, one sentence>.
Gate: <the exact test that must pass>.
Constraints: no Hugging Face model classes in src/quantlens; PyTorch CPU only;
Windows-safe paths (pathlib); fixed seeds.
Stop after the gate passes and show me the pytest output. Do not start the next phase.
Review checklist before every commit
Tests pass on your own machine, not just in the agent's output.
You read the diff and can explain each new function.
No transformers model import inside src/quantlens.
No weights, datasets or .venv committed.
Every number you quote came from a file in results/.
Ask the agent: "Quiz me with 3 questions on what you just wrote."
Windows habits. A new PowerShell tab opens in your home folder, so cd into quantlens first. Use python, not python3, and keep one tab for commands.
Repository layout
One file per concern, and results/ is the only source for any number you quote.
quantlens/
  README.md
  SPEC.md                  contract every agent follows
  AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md GEMINI.md    one line each: "read SPEC.md"
  requirements.txt
  src/quantlens/
    model.py               GPT-2 forward pass (you write)
    weights.py             load Hugging Face weights into your model
    kvcache.py             key/value cache
    generate.py            greedy and top-k sampling
    quant/
      int8.py              weight-only per-tensor and per-channel
      act.py               activation quantization (W8A8)
      smooth.py            SmoothQuant-style rescaling
    eval/
      perplexity.py        WikiText-2, sliding window
      bench.py             latency and memory
      outliers.py          activation statistics
  tests/
    test_logits_match_hf.py
    test_kvcache_equivalence.py
    test_quant_roundtrip.py
  scripts/run_experiments.py
  results/                 JSON or CSV from every run
  reports/                 figures and the PDF report
  .github/workflows/tests.yml
Add .gitignore entries for .venv/, __pycache__/, model weights and the dataset cache.
Build phases and gates
The build runs in seven phases, and each one ends with a test that must pass before the next begins.
[embed: node/58cb49bb-18fb]
Read top to bottom. Stopping after P4 still leaves a complete, tested engine with a weight-quantization study.
Experiment design
Every variant is measured the same way, on the same text, with a hypothesis written down before the run.
Variant
What changes
Question it answers
V0
fp32 baseline
The reference number
V1
Weight-only int8, per-tensor scale
How much does coarse scaling cost?
V2
Weight-only int8, per-channel scale
Does finer scaling recover it?
V3
Weights and activations int8 (W8A8), naive
Do activations break first?
V4
W8A8 plus SmoothQuant-style rescaling
Does the fix work?
V5 (stretch)
Int4 group-wise weights
Where is the real breaking point?

Metrics for every variant
Perplexity: WikiText-2 test set, sliding window, context 1024 (GPT-2's context length), fixed stride, same for all variants.
Memory: bytes of weights, computed from tensor sizes.
Latency: tokens per second at context lengths 128, 512 and 1024, batch size 1, with and without the KV cache. Report the median of 3 runs.
Diagnosis experiments
Layer sensitivity: quantize one transformer block at a time and record the perplexity change per block.
Outlier statistics: for each linear layer input, the ratio of the largest per-channel activation range to the median channel. A few dominant channels would explain naive W8A8 failing.
Hypotheses to write down before running (these are guesses, not results): naive W8A8 degrades more than weight-only int8, and a small number of channels dominate activation range. If either is wrong, report that.
Figures for the report: perplexity by variant, perplexity change by layer, outlier ratio by layer, tokens per second against context length.
Risks and pivots
The most likely failure is a quiet one: the project finishes but shows nothing surprising. Each risk below has a planned response.
Risk
What to do
Naive int8 barely hurts GPT-2 small, so nothing "breaks"
Move the study to int4 group-wise (V5). "GPT-2 small is robust at int8" is still a valid, reportable finding
Quantized CPU inference is not faster (dequantization overhead)
Report it as a finding. Claim memory savings, not speed, unless measured
Logits do not match Hugging Face in Phase 1
Check the usual GPT-2 traps: Conv1D weights are stored transposed, GELU is the tanh approximation, LayerNorm epsilon is 1e-5, the output head shares the token embedding, and the attention mask is causal
The space is crowded (tinygpt, inf-engine and others exist)
Your difference is the question and the diagnosis. Cite those repos in the README; do not copy them
Scope creep (speculative decoding, Triton kernels, int4)
Stretch work starts only after the report draft exists
Time runs short (internship, exams)
Minimum shippable = Phases 0 to 4 plus a short report
Agent writes code you cannot explain
Rewrite it from memory; the core files in Working rules stay yours

Packaging for applications
The README, the report and the CV bullet must all trace back to files in results/; anything else is a claim a reviewer can catch.
README sections: one-paragraph summary, architecture diagram, how to run on Windows and Linux, test instructions, results table, the diagnosis in three sentences, limitations, related work (link the crowded-space repos), license.
Report outline (3 to 4 pages): abstract, setup, method, results, failure analysis, limitations, reproducibility. Write it in Overleaf or Markdown exported to PDF, and commit the PDF.
CI: run fast tests on a tiny random-weight GPT-2 config, and mark the full-weight Hugging Face comparison @pytest.mark.slow so it runs locally. Add a badge only after the first green run.
CV bullet template (fill the brackets only from saved results):
quantlens (Python, PyTorch): Implemented GPT-2 small inference from scratch with a KV cache and int8/W8A8 quantizers; verified logits against Hugging Face to [tolerance] with [N] tests. Diagnosed [finding] using per-layer sensitivity and activation-outlier analysis, and evaluated a SmoothQuant-style fix ([perplexity change]).
Do not claim: speedups you did not measure, novelty, state-of-the-art results, or findings that are not in a saved results file.
Using it in applications: link the repo and the PDF report. Describe it as a diagnosis project: question, method, result, limits. That is the same story your TrackML work already tells.
Day-one checklist
The first hour ends with a public repo, a working environment and one failing test, which is the correct place to start.
Search GitHub and PyPI for quantlens; pick the fallback name if it is taken.
Create the folder, run git init, and create an empty public GitHub repo with the same name.
Create the environment and install: python -m venv .venv, activate it, then pip install torch transformers datasets pytest matplotlib. On Windows the default torch wheel is the CPU build.
Write SPEC.md from this doc: the research question, the "from scratch" boundary, the phases and their gates.
Add the one-line AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md and GEMINI.md pointer files.
Write tests/test_logits_match_hf.py first. It loads Hugging Face gpt2, runs 20 prompts and compares logits with your model, so it fails until Phase 1 works.
Make the first commit and push.
If the browser sign-in for git push hangs, install the GitHub CLI with winget install GitHub.cli, run gh auth login and use the device-code option.
When Phase 1 is done, come back with the test output and I will review it before Phase 2.
